{"title":"Ears","puid":"c-sci-3-gb/wkeeen","path":"/../c-sci-3-gb/topic-03-animals-and-humans/block-01a-human-senses/level-03","type":"normal","keyboardLayout":"standardNATENG","voiceLang":"en","feedback":{"autoplay":false},"voice":"f3_2025","instruction":{"voice":"m2_2025","speaker":true},"lsg":{"width":300},"trainers":[{"trainer":"buttons","shuffleButtons":true,"shuffleAtoms":true,"instruction":{"text":"What can you hear?"},"soundMode":"askSound","atoms":[{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom' gain='0.5'>instrument-guitar-2</voice>","b":"++guitar --piano --drum_kit"},{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom'>lion</voice>","b":"++lion --frog --horse"},{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom'>owl</voice>","b":"++owl --goose --crow"},{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom'>instrument-piano-2</voice>","b":"++piano --trumpet --xylophone"},{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom'>rooster</voice>","b":"++cockerel mouse donkey"},{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom'>zipper closing</voice>","b":"++zip violin printer"},{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom'>thunderstorm01</voice>","b":"++storm waves volcano"},{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom'>drink pour</voice>","b":"++pouring_a_drink shower swimming"},{"a":"<voice voiceLang='de' voice='custom'>apple-bite</voice>","b":"++biting_an_apple banging_a_drum kneading_dough"}]},{"trainer":"gapMulti","finishButton":false,"gapMode":"buttons","instruction":{"text":"Fill in the gaps."},"textAlign":"left","soundMode":"b","atoms":[{"b":"The parts of the ear that we can see are the pinna, the earlobe and the opening of the ((++ear_canal auditory_nerve)). However, most important parts of the ear are inside our head and are not visible from outside.","lsg":"/../c-sci-3-gb/topic-03-animals-and-humans/block-01a-human-senses/lsg/ear-diagram.lsg"},{"b":"The individual tones, noises and speech we hear are made up of vibrating air particles known as sound waves. These sound waves are collected by the pinna and travel through the ((++ear_canal eyelid)) into the ear."},{"b":"The sound waves pass through the ear canal to reach the auditory nerve, which is connected to the brain. The brain translates the signals sent to it so that we can ((++hear see)).","lsg":"/../c-sci-3-gb/topic-03-animals-and-humans/block-01a-human-senses/lsg/earinner.lsg","hint":{"text":"Protected inside the ear, you can find the cochlea, which is responsible for your balance. It can also make you feel dizzy when you spin around quickly! This is because of the fluid in the cochlea’s tubes, which moves as you spin. It keeps going for a while after you stop, giving you the impression that you are still moving.","speaker":true,"autoplay":false,"label":"Did you know?"},"feedback":{"end":{"text":"The human ear can differentiate between up to <b>400,000 tones</b> and can even tell which direction they are coming from.","speaker":true}}}]},{"trainer":"lsg","finishButton":false,"gapMode":"buttons","instruction":{"text":"Label the visible parts of the ear."},"shuffleButtons":true,"combineButtons":true,"removeCorrectButton":true,"soundMode":"b","lsg":{"lsg":"/../c-sci-3-gb/topic-03-animals-and-humans/block-01a-human-senses/lsg/ear-abbildung.lsg","width":400},"atoms":[{"id":"input1","b":"((++pinna))"},{"id":"input2","b":"((++ear_canal))"},{"id":"input4","b":"((++ear_lobe))"}]},{"trainer":"imageButtons","instruction":{"text":"Our hearing can warn us of dangers in our environment. Match the danger to the picture.","speaker":true},"soundMode":"a","shuffleAtoms":true,"shuffleButtons":false,"atoms":[{"a":"cars approaching on the motorway","b":[{"image":"adobe_307057675","correct":true},{"image":"bigstockphoto_251713501"},{"image":"bigstockphoto_213888883"}]},{"a":"a fire alarm ringing","b":[{"image":"adobe_307057675"},{"image":"bigstockphoto_251713501","correct":true},{"image":"bigstockphoto_213888883"}]},{"a":"a barking dog coming from behind","b":[{"image":"adobe_307057675"},{"image":"bigstockphoto_251713501"},{"image":"big